Expanding the Bit Representation of a Number

One common operation is to convert between integers having different word sizes while retaining the same numeric value.
To convert an unsigned number to a larger data type, we can simply add leading zeros to the representation; this operation is known as zero extension see unsigned_expansion.png. For converting a two's-complement number to a larger data type, the rule is to perform a sign extension, adding copies of the most significant bit to the representation see twos_complement.png

As an example, consider the following code:

1  short sx = –12345;	     /* –12345 */
2  unsigned short usx = sx;  /* 53191 */
3  int x = sx;               /* -12345 */
4  unsigned ux = usx;        /* 53191 */
5
6  printf(“sx = %d:\t”, sx);
7  show_bytes((byte_pointer) "sx, sizeof(short));
8  printf(“usx = %u:\t”, usx);
9  show_bytes((byte_pointer) "usx, sizeof(unsigned short));
10 printf(“x = %d:\t”, x);
11 show_bytes((byte_pointer) &x, sizeof(int));
12 printf(“ux = %u:\t”, ux);
13 show_bytes((byte_pointer) &ux, sizeof(unsigned));

When run as a 32–bit program on a big-endian machine that uses a two's-complement representation, this code prints the output

sx = –12345:	cf c7
usx = 53191:	cf c7
x = –12345:	    ff ff cf c7
ux = 53191:     00 00 cf c7

use python3 -c 'n=-5; print(format(n & 0xff, "08b"))' to convert negative decimal numbers to the bit representations in two's-complement. n & 0xff masks to 8 bits i.e. keeps only the lower 16 bits whilst format(..., "08b") prints a binary string with leading zeros upto 8 bits. you can adjust the bit width as needed 

8 bits → 0xFF, "08b"
32 bits → 0xFFFFFFFF, "032b"

